WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.680 --> 00:00:06.898
This is Waladuna Mosque in Jakarta.

00:00:06.898 --> 00:00:09.055
Its&nbsp;roof is in ruins.

00:00:09.055 --> 00:00:10.720
Moss covers the walls. 

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:14.480
And sea water flows
through every corner of it.

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:19.840
The last time people prayed here was&nbsp;in 2001,
back when it was above ground.

00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:23.760
Today, it’s a warning sign.

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:29.150
It signals what could happen to Indonesia’s&nbsp;
capital city if things don’t change soon.

00:00:29.150 --> 00:00:32.987
Because, even though Jakarta
faces the same sea rising levels

00:00:32.987 --> 00:00:35.680
as&nbsp;other coastal cities around the world,

00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:39.920
that’s not quite what we’re looking at here.

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:45.120
Jakarta is sinking.
And it’s&nbsp;been sinking for decades.

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:50.400
These blue areas show just how much&nbsp;
the city has sunk since the 1970s.

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:53.680
The darker the area gets, the more it's dropped.

00:00:53.680 --> 00:00:56.400
Here's how much it's descended today.

00:00:56.400 --> 00:00:59.728
Most of the sinking happens here,
in the north&nbsp;coast,

00:00:59.728 --> 00:01:02.480
where Jakarta meets the Java Sea.

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:06.880
Here, the land is sinking by about 25 cm a year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:09.529
destabilizing the area,
damaging homes,

00:01:09.529 --> 00:01:13.539
and&nbsp;upending people’s lives,
over and over again.

00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:29.920
Many residents here are fishermen, who need&nbsp;
to live by the coast to make a living,

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:36.080
but, further inland, Jakarta’s more than&nbsp;
10 million residents are also at risk.

00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:42.581
A huge portion of the city, and the&nbsp;homes of
millions, could be underwater&nbsp;by 2050.

00:01:42.581 --> 00:01:46.320
Jakarta sits on a&nbsp;
swampy plain, on low coastal land.

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:49.660
It has 13 rivers that drain through it.

00:01:49.660 --> 00:01:51.772
But the reason the city is sinking

00:01:51.772 --> 00:01:57.309
is actually&nbsp;that most people here
don’t have enough water.

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:01.920
Most Jakartans lack access
to clean, piped water.

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:05.840
Instead, they get their water&nbsp;
by digging wells like this one.

00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:10.320
The pumps go deep into the ground to&nbsp;
extract the water stored in aquifers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:13.271
underground layers of rock
that hold groundwater.

00:02:13.271 --> 00:02:16.860
The porous spaces of the rock
are filled with it.

00:02:16.860 --> 00:02:20.952
Multiply this by a few million,&nbsp;
and you have a problem.

00:02:20.952 --> 00:02:23.541
Think of the rock as a soaked&nbsp;sponge:

00:02:23.541 --> 00:02:26.831
the more water is extracted,
the more it deflates,

00:02:26.831 --> 00:02:32.710
causing the soil to compact&nbsp;and collapse,
and the ground above it to sink.

00:02:32.710 --> 00:02:35.635
Pumps alone shouldn’t be able to do this.

00:02:35.635 --> 00:02:39.348
While some layers of earth
will never recover their water,

00:02:39.348 --> 00:02:42.684
aquifers&nbsp;are usually
refilled naturally when it rains.

00:02:42.684 --> 00:02:46.644
But in Jakarta, that’s
becoming increasingly rare.

00:02:46.644 --> 00:02:50.057
For decades, Jakarta has been
developing at&nbsp;a fast pace,

00:02:50.057 --> 00:02:52.660
and is now covered in concrete.

00:02:52.660 --> 00:02:58.293
So the rainfall that would usually fill&nbsp;up
the aquifers isn’t being absorbed.

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:05.602
It’s gotten so bad that in
coastal areas prone to flooding,

00:03:05.602 --> 00:03:08.034
like the fishing community Muara Baru,

00:03:08.034 --> 00:03:12.457
people have built makeshift bridges&nbsp;
to move through their neighborhoods.

00:03:12.457 --> 00:03:14.197
Combined with sea level rise,

00:03:14.197 --> 00:03:19.840
it's also made floods during high tide
and rainy seasons much more dangerous.

00:03:19.840 --> 00:03:24.129
Like in 2007, when Jakarta
experienced one&nbsp;of the worst floods

00:03:24.129 --> 00:03:26.220
in its modern history.

00:03:26.220 --> 00:03:31.164
A storm and high tide caused rivers
and canals&nbsp;around the city to overflow,

00:03:31.164 --> 00:03:32.637
killing 80 people.

00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:42.640
Maksim has already lost
his home to the sinking,

00:03:42.640 --> 00:03:45.037
and now sleeps on his fishing boat.

00:03:45.037 --> 00:03:49.138
And&nbsp;Nondho has had to rebuild
his home several times.

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:05.820
Groundwater pumping is putting&nbsp;
Jakarta’s survival at risk.

00:04:05.820 --> 00:04:09.971
But to understand how it got
into this situation&nbsp;to begin with,

00:04:09.971 --> 00:04:13.389
you have to go back centuries.

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:17.920
In the 1600s, when European&nbsp;
powers were colonizing the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.920 --> 00:04:22.240
the Dutch took over what was then&nbsp;
the port town of Jayakarta.

00:04:22.240 --> 00:04:26.293
They razed it to the ground,
and in its place, built&nbsp;Batavia:

00:04:26.293 --> 00:04:28.480
a headquarters for their growing empire.

00:04:28.480 --> 00:04:30.560
They began to rule over the Indonesian,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.560 --> 00:04:35.120
Chinese, Indian, and Arab people&nbsp;
who had lived there for centuries,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.120 --> 00:04:37.444
and built their new city
in the Dutch style,

00:04:37.444 --> 00:04:41.244
with narrow townhouses
along a grid of canals.

00:04:41.244 --> 00:04:47.040
The canals were used for trade, defense,&nbsp;
and to make Batavia feel like a Dutch city.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:49.372
But look at Batavia from above,

00:04:49.372 --> 00:04:53.355
and you can&nbsp;see the city grid
served a darker purpose, too.

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:19.875
If you look closely, you'll notice&nbsp;
that there aren’t many bridges

00:05:19.875 --> 00:05:23.160
between the two sides,
or between the&nbsp;blocks.

00:05:23.160 --> 00:05:27.250
This was by design.
The Dutch were outnumbered.

00:05:27.250 --> 00:05:31.600
So, in order to&nbsp;control the local
population, they divided it.

00:05:31.600 --> 00:05:33.327
It looked like this.

00:05:33.327 --> 00:05:38.386
Pretty much every&nbsp;group was
confined to their city quarter.

00:05:38.386 --> 00:05:42.899
The Dutch ruled over the local
population like&nbsp;this for over a century.

00:05:42.899 --> 00:05:47.120
But that&nbsp;began to change
in the mid-1700s.

00:05:47.120 --> 00:05:51.600
Because the Dutch didn’t properly maintain&nbsp;
the canals, they began to deteriorate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.600 --> 00:05:54.800
and sediment from earthquakes&nbsp;
blocked the flow of water.

00:05:54.800 --> 00:05:59.514
The water in the canals turned&nbsp;
stagnant, and soon, deadly.

00:06:13.440 --> 00:06:15.392
As disease spread through the canals,

00:06:15.392 --> 00:06:17.920
the&nbsp;wealthier Dutch moved
south of Batavia,

00:06:17.920 --> 00:06:21.760
where they began to develop a new&nbsp;
colonial administrative center.

00:06:21.760 --> 00:06:26.940
But, despite the death and disease, the&nbsp;Dutch
continued to leave the canals&nbsp;untreated.

00:06:26.940 --> 00:06:30.080
Instead, they began to use piped water.

00:06:30.080 --> 00:06:34.640
In the 1870s, they developed the&nbsp;
first centralized water supply,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.640 --> 00:06:37.760
with iron pipes to
distribute water to homes.

00:06:37.760 --> 00:06:41.680
The pipes provided clean drinking&nbsp;
water and indoor bathrooms.

00:06:41.680 --> 00:06:46.640
But the pipes were concentrated in these&nbsp;
areas, where the Dutch had moved to.

00:06:46.640 --> 00:06:50.880
The indigenous population was left in&nbsp;
informal settlements, called "kampongs,"

00:06:50.880 --> 00:06:52.454
far from the piped water.

00:06:52.454 --> 00:06:57.163
And this created&nbsp;a new
kind of division in the city.

00:07:09.280 --> 00:07:13.440
Native residents had to rely&nbsp;
on street vendors for water.

00:07:13.440 --> 00:07:18.240
But most often, they were forced to get&nbsp;
their water from the neglected canals.

00:07:18.240 --> 00:07:22.560
It took decades before pipes were&nbsp;
finally built in these communities.

00:07:22.560 --> 00:07:26.952
And when they were, it would&nbsp;
only be a few public standpipes.

00:07:44.480 --> 00:07:47.489
This continued through 1949.

00:07:47.489 --> 00:07:55.360
After an armed conflict, the Dutch finally
recognized&nbsp;Indonesia’s independence, and left.

00:07:55.360 --> 00:07:58.960
The legacy they left behind
was a sprawling city,

00:07:58.960 --> 00:08:02.583
built on marshland,
and segregated by water&nbsp;access,

00:08:02.583 --> 00:08:05.949
that, now, Jakartans had to deal with.

00:08:05.949 --> 00:08:10.080
Over the next decades,&nbsp;
Jakarta’s population skyrocketed.

00:08:10.080 --> 00:08:14.424
More people required more housing,
more stores,&nbsp;and more streets.

00:08:14.424 --> 00:08:17.783
And the city expanded fast.

00:08:17.783 --> 00:08:22.480
But its water infrastructure still&nbsp;
doesn’t serve the majority of the city.

00:08:22.480 --> 00:08:26.480
This chart shows how much of&nbsp;
Jakarta’s population has piped water.

00:08:26.480 --> 00:08:31.520
It was 12 percent in the 50s, and&nbsp;
is still under 50 percent today.

00:08:31.520 --> 00:08:34.461
Many of the people without
access to piped&nbsp;water

00:08:34.461 --> 00:08:38.989
have no other choice but to keep
pumping groundwater&nbsp;to survive.

00:08:38.989 --> 00:08:42.149
And the city continues to sink.

00:08:42.149 --> 00:08:43.920
The situation has gotten so bad,

00:08:43.920 --> 00:08:48.480
the Indonesian government has talked&nbsp;
about moving the capital, from Jakarta,

00:08:48.480 --> 00:08:50.720
to the neighboring island of Borneo.

00:08:50.720 --> 00:08:56.189
But that won’t help the millions&nbsp;
of people living in Jakarta.

00:08:56.560 --> 00:09:00.480
To save the city, in 2014, the&nbsp;
government announced a project,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.480 --> 00:09:03.159
in collaboration with a
Dutch architecture firm,

00:09:03.159 --> 00:09:07.200
to build and reinforce
120 km of seawalls,

00:09:07.200 --> 00:09:10.953
to stop the water from flooding&nbsp;
the land as it sinks.

00:09:10.953 --> 00:09:15.253
But so far, only these 10 kilometers
have been&nbsp;reinforced.

00:09:15.253 --> 00:09:18.015
Like this one in Muara Baru.

00:09:18.015 --> 00:09:22.786
The problem is that, just like the rest&nbsp;
of Jakarta, the seawall is sinking.

00:09:31.840 --> 00:09:35.847
The project also includes an
ambitious&nbsp;$40 billion plan

00:09:35.847 --> 00:09:39.528
to build a 38 km wall,
shaped like a massive bird,

00:09:39.528 --> 00:09:41.520
to protect the coast from flooding.

00:09:41.520 --> 00:09:45.360
But this project could take&nbsp;
up to 30 years to complete.

00:09:45.360 --> 00:09:49.840
And by then, Jakarta could have&nbsp;
lost most of its coastal land.

00:10:03.965 --> 00:10:08.240
Jakarta will continues to sink until&nbsp;
groundwater stops being pumped.

00:10:08.240 --> 00:10:11.317
And groundwater will
continue to be pumped

00:10:11.317 --> 00:10:14.514
until the government
provides an alternative.

00:10:14.514 --> 00:10:17.440
This has been done before.
In the 1950s,

00:10:17.440 --> 00:10:21.920
Tokyo managed to stop severe&nbsp;
sinking by providing piped water.

00:10:21.920 --> 00:10:25.680
Taipei, Shanghai, Bangkok,&nbsp;
are other cities in the region&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.680 --> 00:10:28.770
that have managed to
stop their cities&nbsp;from sinking.

00:10:28.770 --> 00:10:31.546
But time is running out.

00:10:42.960 --> 00:10:47.093
Jakarta has been free of Dutch rule
for&nbsp;a little over 70 years now.

00:10:47.093 --> 00:10:50.204
But the way the Dutch
built their city, carved it up,

00:10:50.204 --> 00:10:54.225
and&nbsp;restricted its water,
plagues it to this day.

00:10:54.225 --> 00:10:56.811
Jakarta is sinking into the sea.

00:10:56.811 --> 00:11:02.394
And, until its&nbsp;government figures out how
to provide clean, piped water for its citizens,

00:11:02.394 --> 00:11:05.112
that will continue to&nbsp;be its reality.

00:11:05.112 --> 00:11:08.048
For as long as it’s still here.

